Subject: [PATCH] input: gemini-nt36xxx: take the panel size from the device tree

The axes are registered at probe. The firmware cannot be asked at probe. So
the axes were registered from a fallback that is wrong for this board.

This is a TDDI part: the touch controller shares the panel's power domain and
does not answer on I2C until the panel is prepared. Measured on build #89:

  [ 0.851] gemini-nt36xxx 4-0062: firmware info read failed (-6); assuming 1080x1920
  [ 5.483] gemini-nt36xxx 4-0062: page select failed: -6
  [ 6.677] gemini-nt36xxx 4-0062: firmware reports 1080x2160

Probe runs at 0.85 s; the controller does not ACK until 6.68 s. The later read
updated ts->max_x/max_y but nothing ever revised the registered axis, so the
driver reported over 0..2160 while having declared 0..1920. libinput
normalises against the declaration, so every touch was stretched by
2160/1920 = 1.125 and anything past x=1920 was delivered to no surface at all
-- the right-hand 11% of the screen dead, which is where the taskbar's wifi
and volume icons live.

Retrying inside probe cannot fix this; it would have to spin for six seconds.
-EPROBE_DEFER cannot either: deferred probe is retried when other drivers
bind, and nothing binds at the moment DRM prepares the panel, so the driver
could sit deferred indefinitely.

The panel geometry is a property of the board and is known at probe. Take it
from the standard touchscreen-size-x/y properties and use the firmware read
only to confirm it, warning loudly if the two disagree rather than adopting a
size the registered axis can no longer follow.

The board's other, unused touch controller node (solomon,ssd2092-touch) has
carried touchscreen-size-x/y = 1080/2160 all along, derived independently from
the vendor driver's mapping -- so the geometry has two agreeing sources.

Verified that dtbs_check actually validates this node rather than skipping it:
inserting a bogus property produces
"touchscreen@62 (novatek,nt36xxx-ts): Unevaluated properties are not allowed",
and removing it returns the DTB to 0 warnings.

--- a/drivers/input/touchscreen/gemini-nt36xxx.c
+++ b/drivers/input/touchscreen/gemini-nt36xxx.c
@@ -76,7 +76,12 @@
 #define NVT_MAX_FINGERS		10
 #define NVT_MAX_PRESSURE	1000	/* TOUCH_FORCE_NUM */
 
-/* Fallbacks if the firmware-info read fails; the vendor uses the same. */
+/*
+ * Last-resort fallbacks if neither the device tree nor the firmware says.
+ * The vendor uses the same, and on this board they are WRONG: the panel is
+ * 1080x2160. Prefer touchscreen-size-x/y in the device tree -- see the long
+ * comment above nvt_read_fw_info().
+ */
 #define NVT_DEFAULT_MAX_X	1080
 #define NVT_DEFAULT_MAX_Y	1920
 
@@ -177,32 +182,73 @@
 	msleep(50);
 }
 
+/*
+ * THE FIRMWARE CANNOT BE ASKED AT PROBE TIME, AND THE AXES ARE REGISTERED AT
+ * PROBE TIME.
+ *
+ * This is a TDDI part: the touch controller shares the panel's power domain,
+ * so it does not answer on I2C until the panel is prepared. Measured on this
+ * board (build #89):
+ *
+ *   [ 0.851] firmware info read failed (-6); assuming 1080x1920   <- probe
+ *   [ 5.483] page select failed: -6
+ *   [ 6.677] firmware reports 1080x2160                           <- panel up
+ *
+ * Probe runs at 0.85 s and the controller does not ACK until 6.68 s. So the
+ * probe-time read fails, the axes get registered from the fallback, and the
+ * later read updates ts->max_x/max_y without ever revising the registered
+ * range. The driver then REPORTS over 0..2160 while having DECLARED 0..1920,
+ * libinput normalises against the declaration, and the right-hand 11% of the
+ * screen is dead to touch -- which is how this was found (#76).
+ *
+ * Retrying inside probe cannot fix it; it would have to spin for six seconds.
+ * -EPROBE_DEFER cannot fix it either: deferred probe is retried when other
+ * drivers bind, and nothing binds at the moment DRM prepares the panel, so the
+ * driver could sit deferred forever.
+ *
+ * The panel geometry is a property of the BOARD, not something that has to be
+ * discovered. Take it from the standard touchscreen-size-x/y device-tree
+ * properties, which are known at probe, and use the firmware read only to
+ * confirm it.
+ */
 static void nvt_read_fw_info(struct nvt_ts *ts)
 {
+	u32 fw_x, fw_y;
 	u8 buf[17] = {};
 	int ret;
 
 	ret = nvt_read(ts, NVT_EVENT_FWINFO, buf, sizeof(buf));
 	if (ret) {
 		dev_warn(&ts->client->dev,
-			 "firmware info read failed (%d); assuming %ux%u\n",
-			 ret, NVT_DEFAULT_MAX_X, NVT_DEFAULT_MAX_Y);
-		ts->max_x = NVT_DEFAULT_MAX_X;
-		ts->max_y = NVT_DEFAULT_MAX_Y;
+			 "firmware info read failed (%d); keeping %ux%u\n",
+			 ret, ts->max_x, ts->max_y);
 		return;
 	}
 
-	/* Panel resolution is reported by the firmware, not assumed. */
-	ts->max_x = (buf[4] << 8) | buf[5];
-	ts->max_y = (buf[6] << 8) | buf[7];
+	fw_x = (buf[4] << 8) | buf[5];
+	fw_y = (buf[6] << 8) | buf[7];
 
-	if (!ts->max_x || !ts->max_y) {
-		ts->max_x = NVT_DEFAULT_MAX_X;
-		ts->max_y = NVT_DEFAULT_MAX_Y;
+	if (!fw_x || !fw_y) {
+		dev_warn(&ts->client->dev,
+			 "firmware reported a zero dimension (%ux%u); keeping %ux%u\n",
+			 fw_x, fw_y, ts->max_x, ts->max_y);
+		return;
 	}
 
-	dev_info(&ts->client->dev, "firmware reports %ux%u\n",
-		 ts->max_x, ts->max_y);
+	/*
+	 * Do not adopt a different size here: the input device's axis limits
+	 * were registered from ts->max_x/max_y at probe and cannot be revised
+	 * behind a client that has already opened it. Say so loudly instead --
+	 * a mismatch means the device tree is wrong, and silently reporting
+	 * outside the declared range is the bug this comment exists for.
+	 */
+	if (fw_x != ts->max_x || fw_y != ts->max_y)
+		dev_warn(&ts->client->dev,
+			 "firmware reports %ux%u but the axes were registered as %ux%u; fix touchscreen-size-x/y\n",
+			 fw_x, fw_y, ts->max_x, ts->max_y);
+	else
+		dev_dbg(&ts->client->dev, "firmware confirms %ux%u\n",
+			fw_x, fw_y);
 }
 
 static void nvt_ts_poll(struct input_dev *input)
@@ -411,6 +457,18 @@
 	ts->swap_xy = device_property_read_bool(dev, "touchscreen-swapped-x-y");
 	ts->invert_y = device_property_read_bool(dev, "touchscreen-inverted-y");
 
+	/*
+	 * Before the reset, because nvt_read_fw_info() only confirms these --
+	 * it cannot supply them at probe time. See the comment above it.
+	 */
+	if (device_property_read_u32(dev, "touchscreen-size-x", &ts->max_x))
+		ts->max_x = NVT_DEFAULT_MAX_X;
+	if (device_property_read_u32(dev, "touchscreen-size-y", &ts->max_y))
+		ts->max_y = NVT_DEFAULT_MAX_Y;
+	if (!ts->max_x || !ts->max_y)
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, -EINVAL,
+				     "touchscreen-size-x/y must be non-zero\n");
+
 	nvt_reset(ts);
 	nvt_read_fw_info(ts);
 
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda.dts
@@ -646,6 +646,19 @@
 		touchscreen-swapped-x-y;
 		touchscreen-inverted-y;
 		/*
+		 * The panel's native resolution, in the panel's own orientation
+		 * (the driver applies the swap above when it registers the
+		 * axes). This is NOT belt-and-braces for the firmware read: it
+		 * is the only source available in time. Being a TDDI part, the
+		 * controller does not answer on I2C until the panel is
+		 * prepared -- 6.68 s into boot, against a probe at 0.85 s -- so
+		 * the axes would otherwise be registered from the driver's
+		 * 1080x1920 fallback while the hardware reports up to 2160, and
+		 * the right-hand 11% of the screen is dead to touch (#76).
+		 */
+		touchscreen-size-x = <1080>;
+		touchscreen-size-y = <2160>;
+		/*
 		 * This is a TDDI part: the touch controller shares the panel's
 		 * die and its power domain, so the panel's prepare/unprepare is
 		 * this chip's power sequence. Naming the panel here makes the
